member.summary:
Issue #4315: THE rule for when the translator may omit the != nil guard that a bare recursive pattern opens with. C# tests the subject for null (with Nullable disabled or enabled alike: a declared-non-null member is null whenever it was never assigned, came from oblivious code or was null!-initialized), so the guard is omitted only when G# itself proves the value non-nil, or when emitting it is rejected outright:
a non-nullable value type (never null; Nullable<T> is not one);
a params array subject (a G# variadic always materializes an
array; the guard is GS0523);
a range slice of a built-in array or string (xs[1..^1]), which slicing never
makes nil (a custom Slice method can return null, so it keeps the guard);
a declared bare-slice slot, i.e. a field/property read registered
by RegisterPatternMemberShape (G# zero value is an empty slice, so the guard is dead; GS0523). Anything else keeps the guard, including a list element (new int[1][] holds a CLR null), a promoted T? local or member (int[] a = null!) and an analyzer-API T? receiver. Both the emitter and the read-count classifier call this, so they cannot disagree.

Lowers a C# positive type-pattern guard if (receiver is T t) { … } to a smart-cast-friendly G# local that preserves the declaration variable's identity.let t T? = receiver as T // reference target if t != nil { … }
let __spill0 = receiver // value target var t T if __spill0 is T { t = T(__spill0); … }
Value-pattern binders use a separate typed local so their reads do not rely on flow narrowing through an enclosing try/block. Reference targets retain the nullable-local lowering used for G# nil narrowing.
Issue #4173 round 3: adapts a guard-hoist's plain let t T? = receiver as T; if t != nil scheme for one of the three shared- node analyzer types ( / ), which every guard-hoist site (, , ) otherwise reaches via a direct, unguarded this.MapTypeSyntax(typeSyntax) call that bypasses the discriminator/predicate entirely — the exact bug class this round fixes, reintroduced at the hoist level if left unhandled. CAE has no castable G# type at all (its faithful rewrite is the ALREADY-NULLABLE AsNullConditionalHop predicate, which drops straight into the existing hoist shape unchanged); a plain-access type keeps the ordinary as-cast but needs an extra IsNullConditional guard conjunct, which tells the caller to add (with the polarity appropriate to a POSITIVE guard — a negated caller inverts it).

Issue #3352: emits C#'s body-scoped positive loop-pattern binding as native G# while let when the condition is exactly a non-null binding or type-test binding, optionally followed by spill-free && guards:while (Next() is string text && text.Length > 0) { body }
            // becomes
            while let text = Next() as string {
            if !(text.Length > 0) { break }
            body
            }
The native loop owns re-evaluation and continue/break routing, so the old let __scrutineeN body prologue is unnecessary.

Lowers a while/do-while whose condition carries an is-pattern clause that would otherwise duplicate a side-effecting scrutinee or leak a binder the G# loop body cannot see (issue #914).
C# allows a loop condition such as M(out var n) is Frame child and not EmptyFrame, binding child/n for the loop body. G# has no and/not pattern combinators (only &&/!), so the combinator lowering re-emits the scrutinee per sub-test — re-running the call and re-declaring out var n (→ GS0102). Pattern/out-var bindings that cannot use native while let are also invisible in an ordinary G# while body (GS0125).
The condition is split on its top-level && clauses. The leading side-effect-free clauses stay the real loop condition; from the first clause that binds or duplicates a side-effecting scrutinee onward, each clause is hoisted to the top of the loop body — the scrutinee evaluated once into a local, the remaining must-hold tests converted to if !test { break } guards:while a && b && M(out var n) is Frame child and not EmptyFrame { … }
            // becomes
            while a && b {
            let child = M(out var n)
            if child is EmptyFrame { break }
            …
            }
Returns false (keep the plain while cond { } form) when no clause needs hoisting, so simple loops are unaffected.
